.TH MU2EDAQ\-DISKWATCHER 1 "September 2026" "mu2edaq-diskwatcher 1.3.0" "Mu2e DAQ Utilities"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \- file modification-time, free-space and file-size monitor
with embedded web dashboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIADDR\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-poll\-interval\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-default\-delay\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-daemon\fR]
[\fB\-\-run\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-pid\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-peer\fR \fIURL\fR ...]
[\fB\-\-no\-peers\fR]
[\fB\-\-peer\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-peer\-interval\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher
monitors files and directories listed in a YAML configuration file for three
independent conditions, each with its own alarm states and its own dashboard
page:
.TP
.B Staleness
The path has not been modified within its configured
.BR delay .
A background thread stats each configured path; if the elapsed time since the
last modification exceeds the threshold, the entry is flagged
.IR stale .
An entry with no
.B delay
is reported as
.I unmonitored
and never raises a staleness alarm.
.TP
.B Free space
The filesystem holding a watched directory has fallen below a threshold given
in the entry's
.B space
block.
See
.BR "DISK SPACE MONITORING" .
.TP
.B File size
A watched file is zero length, or has grown past a threshold given in the
entry's
.B size
block.
See
.BR "FILE SIZE MONITORING" .
.PP
Entries may specify an
.B ssh
block to watch paths on remote hosts.
A single SSH round trip per entry retrieves the modification time, the size,
and (for directories) the filesystem usage.
All entries are polled concurrently using a thread pool so that a slow or
unreachable host does not delay the local checks.
.PP
Several instances can be federated.
A
.B peers
list names other diskwatchers whose
.B /api/state
is fetched over HTTP; their entries appear on this instance's dashboards
grouped under each peer's connection details.
See
.BR PEERS .
.PP
An embedded Flask web server serves a Bootstrap dashboard at
.BR http://\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR/ .
The three live pages
.RB ( / ", " /space " and " /sizes )
each show summary cards counting the entries in every state, followed by a
sortable table.
They refresh by polling their JSON endpoint at a user-selectable interval
(default: 5 s); the chosen interval is remembered across pages.
See
.B WEB ENDPOINTS
for the full list of pages and API endpoints.
.PP
The program may optionally be run as a POSIX background daemon (see
.BR \-\-daemon ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
YAML configuration file to load.
Defaults to
.BR diskwatcher.yaml .
If the file is named explicitly \(em by this option or by
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_CONFIG
\(em and does not exist, the program exits with an error.
A missing
.B diskwatcher.yaml
is tolerated, and built-in defaults are used.
.TP
.BR \-\-host " " \fIADDR\fR
Bind address for the web server.
Overrides
.B watcher.web_host
in the configuration file.
Default:
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
TCP port for the web server.
Overrides
.BR watcher.web_port .
Default:
.BR 5002 .
.TP
.BR \-\-poll\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds between polls.
Overrides
.BR watcher.poll_interval .
Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.BR \-\-default\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fR
Staleness threshold applied to entries that specify neither
.B delay
nor a
.BR space / size
block.
Overrides
.BR watcher.default_delay .
Default:
.BR 300 .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Detach from the terminal and run in the background (POSIX only).
See
.BR "DAEMON MODE" .
.TP
.BR \-\-run\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Per-node directory for everything the process writes.
Overrides
.BR watcher.run_dir .
Default:
.BR run/{host} .
See
.BR "PER-NODE RUNTIME FILES" .
.TP
.BR \-\-pid\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the daemon's process ID to
.IR FILE .
The file is removed at exit.
Default in daemon mode:
.BR {run_dir}/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.pid .
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Redirect the daemon's standard output and standard error to
.IR FILE ,
opened in append mode.
Default in daemon mode:
.BR {run_dir}/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.log .
.PP
All three paths accept the placeholders
.BR {host} ", " {hostname} ", " {port} ", " {user}
and (except
.BR \-\-run\-dir " itself) " {run_dir} ;
see
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.conf (5).
.TP
.BR \-\-peer " " \fIURL\fR
Add a peer diskwatcher to aggregate; may be repeated.
The scheme may be omitted
.RB ( dl\-01:5002 ).
When given at all, the command-line list
.I replaces
the
.B peers
list from the configuration file and from
.BR MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PEERS .
A value that is not an HTTP(S) URL is reported and skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-peers
Ignore every configured peer.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
HTTP timeout per peer fetch.
Overrides
.BR watcher.peer_timeout .
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds between peer fetches.
Overrides
.BR watcher.peer_interval .
Default: the poll interval.
.TP
.BR \-\-discover\-peers ", " \-\-no\-discover\-peers
Turn discovery of peers with
.BR mu2edaq\-discovery (1)
on or off, overriding
.BR peers.discover.enabled .
.TP
.BR \-\-discover\-filter " " \fIKEY\fR=\fIGLOB\fR
Discovery filter, replacing
.BR peers.discover.filter ;
may be repeated.
Keys are
.BR app ", " name " and " host ;
.B app
defaults to
.BR diskwatcher .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log additional detail about remote probes, poll cycles and peer fetches.
Without it, one line is logged each time a peer's connection state changes.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION PRECEDENCE
Settings are resolved in four layers, each overriding the one before it:
.PP
.nf
    built-in defaults
    the YAML configuration file
    environment variables    (see ENVIRONMENT)
    command-line options
.fi
.PP
An option that is not supplied at a given layer leaves the previous layer's
value untouched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every setting may be overridden by an environment variable named after it with
the prefix
.BR MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_ .
A value that cannot be parsed is reported as a warning and ignored; it never
prevents startup.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_CONFIG
Path to the YAML configuration file.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_WEB_HOST
Web server bind address.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_WEB_PORT
Web server port.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_POLL_INTERVAL
Seconds between polls.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_DEFAULT_DELAY
Default staleness threshold.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_DAEMON
Run as a daemon.
Accepts
.BR 1 ", " true ", " yes ", " on
and their negatives.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_RUN_DIR
Per-node runtime directory.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PID_FILE
Daemon PID file.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_LOG_FILE
Daemon log file.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_VERBOSE
Verbose logging.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PEERS
Peer URLs separated by commas or whitespace, replacing the configuration
file's
.B peers
list.
If any one of them is not an HTTP(S) URL the whole variable is reported and
ignored.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PEER_TIMEOUT
Seconds per peer fetch.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PEER_INTERVAL
Seconds between peer fetches.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_DISCOVER_PEERS
Discover peers; a boolean.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_DISCOVER_FILTER
Discovery filter as
.IR KEY = GLOB
pairs separated by commas or whitespace, e.g.
.BR "host=mu2e-dl-*" .
.PP
The start and stop scripts additionally read
.B CRS_PORT_HTTP
(mapped to
.BR \-\-port ),
.BR CRS_STOP_TIMEOUT ,
the seconds to wait after
.B SIGTERM
before sending
.BR SIGKILL ,
default 10, and
.BR DW_RUN_DIR ,
the per-node run directory, default
.BR ./run/ \fIshort-hostname\fR.
See
.BR "START AND STOP SCRIPTS" .
.SH SIZE UNITS
Thresholds in both
.B space
and
.B size
blocks accept a number with an optional unit suffix.
Suffixes are case-insensitive; a bare number means bytes.
.PP
.nf
    SI, powers of 1000:   B  KB/K  MB/M  GB/G  TB/T  PB/P
    IEC, powers of 1024:      KiB   MiB   GiB   TiB   PiB
.fi
.PP
So
.B 500 GiB
is 536870912000 bytes while
.B 500 GB
is 500000000000 bytes.
.PP
The dashboards always
.I display
byte counts in IEC units, so a threshold configured as
.B 2TB
is shown as
.BR "1.8 TiB" .
That is the same quantity written the other way round, not a rounding error.
.PP
A percentage such as
.B 10%
is accepted in
.B space
blocks only, where it means
.I ten percent of total capacity still free.
A file has no total capacity, so a percentage in a
.B size
block is rejected: that one threshold is dropped with a warning and the
remaining thresholds in the block continue to apply.
.SH DISK SPACE MONITORING
A
.B space
block on a
.B paths
entry enables free-space monitoring for the filesystem holding that directory.
The entry then appears on the
.B /space
dashboard and in
.BR /api/space .
.PP
Thresholds state how much space must remain
.IR free ,
so they descend:
.BR "warning > critical > full" .
Each threshold is optional; any subset works.
A threshold is inclusive on the bad side, so free space exactly equal to
.B warning
is already WARNING.
.TP
.B GOOD
Free space is above every configured threshold.
.TP
.B WARNING
free \(<= warning
.TP
.B CRITICAL
free \(<= critical
.TP
.B FULL
free \(<= full
.TP
.B MISSING
The directory could not be read.
.TP
.B UNKNOWN
The directory exists but its filesystem could not be measured.
.PP
Thresholds that do not escalate in the expected order are reported as a warning
at startup and marked on the dashboard, but are never silently corrected; the
effect is simply that some alarm states become unreachable.
When a block mixes percentages with absolute sizes the ordering depends on the
filesystem's capacity, so it is checked at poll time instead and reported
through the
.B space_limits_ordered
field.
.SH FILE SIZE MONITORING
A
.B size
block on a
.B files
entry enables size monitoring for that file.
The entry then appears on the
.B /sizes
dashboard and in
.BR /api/sizes .
.PP
Thresholds state how large the file may grow, so they ascend:
.BR "warning < critical < full" .
.B max
is accepted as a synonym for
.BR full .
Each threshold is optional.
.TP
.B GOOD
The file is below every configured threshold.
.TP
.B EMPTY
The file is zero length.
This is checked
.I before
any threshold, so a truncated file alarms whatever its limits say.
Set
.B allow_empty: true
in the block to suppress it, for files that are legitimately empty most of the
time.
.TP
.B WARNING
size \(>= warning
.TP
.B CRITICAL
size \(>= critical
.TP
.B FULL
size \(>= full
.TP
.B MISSING
The file could not be read.
.PP
A block containing only
.B allow_empty: false
and no thresholds is valid and useful: it means "alarm if this file is ever
zero bytes".
.SH PEERS
A
.B peers
list in the configuration file (or
.BR \-\-peer )
names other diskwatcher instances.
A background thread fetches each peer's
.B /api/state
every
.B peer_interval
seconds, concurrently, with a per-fetch timeout, so a slow or dead peer never
delays the local poll or the web server.
The peer's entries then appear on
.BR / ", " /space " and " /sizes
as one collapsible group per peer, after the local host groups, headed by the
peer's label, its URL (linking to the same page there), the hostname and
version it reports, and the age of the data.
The summary cards count local entries plus those of every reachable peer.
.PP
Three properties of the design matter to an operator:
.TP
.B States are the peer's own.
The peer evaluated its thresholds against a filesystem it can see; this
instance shows the result and does not recompute it.
.TP
.B Aggregation is one hop.
A peer is asked for its own entries only, so two instances may list each other
and nothing is shown twice.
A peer whose URL turns out to be this very process is recognised by its
.B instance_id
and refused.
.TP
.B An outage dims, it does not erase.
While a peer is unreachable, its entries from the last good fetch stay on
screen dimmed, with the error and the time they were last current, and its
group is held open.
.PP
On the Disk Space page the WARNING, CRITICAL, FULL and UNAVAILABLE summary
cards are filters: clicking one shows only entries in that category (local and
peer alike) while the cards keep their full counts; clicking it again, the
TOTAL card or the
.I Reset filter
link clears it.
.IR "Expand all" ", " "Collapse all" " and " Auto
in the table header roll every host and peer group up or open together, or
return them to following their own health.
Those entries are excluded from the summary cards and from every
.B aggregate
in the JSON API.
An unreachable peer is reported in
.B /api/health
under
.B peers
but does not change this instance's own
.BR status .
.PP
Peers may also be found rather than listed: a
.B peers.discover
block scans for other diskwatchers with
.BR mu2edaq\-discovery (1)
every scan interval and federates whatever answers, merging with the static
list (a static entry wins on the same URL).
Discovery decides which peers are listed; HTTP decides whether each is up.
A peer that stops answering is kept, and still fetched, until it has been
unseen for the grace period, so one lost multicast reply never blanks a node's
rows.
Each peer's origin is shown on the dashboards and reported as
.B source
in the API.
The responder advertises the node's fully qualified name, so that name must
resolve from the node doing the discovering; where it does not, the peer shows
as unreachable with the offending URL in its heading.
See
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.conf (5),
section
.BR "THE peers SECTION" .
.PP
Every JSON list endpoint returns local entries only unless
.B ?peers=1
is added; see
.BR "JSON endpoints" .
.SH PER-NODE RUNTIME FILES
The checkout this program runs from is commonly on NFS, shared by several DAQ
nodes, each running its own copy against its own disks.
Everything a copy writes is therefore kept in a per-node run directory,
.B watcher.run_dir
(default
.BR run/{host} ,
where
.B {host}
is the short hostname), so that no two nodes read each other's pid file or
append to one log.
The pid file and log default to
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.pid
and
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.log
inside it, in daemon mode only; a foreground run behaves as before.
Any history or database a future version keeps belongs there too.
.PP
The start and stop scripts use the same directory
.RB ( ./run/ \fIshort-hostname\fR,
or
.BR DW_RUN_DIR ,
or
.BR \-\-run\-dir ),
and pass it to the daemon, so what the script looks for is what the daemon
wrote.
Both scripts also read the pre-1.3.0 location
.B ./diskwatcher.pid
so that a daemon started by an older release is still found and replaced.
The process-table sweep needs no such care: it only ever sees processes on
the node it runs on.
.PP
Placeholders are listed in
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.conf (5),
section
.BR "PATH PLACEHOLDERS" .
.SH DAEMON MODE
With
.BR \-\-daemon ,
the process detaches using the POSIX double-fork idiom: it forks, calls
.BR setsid (2),
forks again, and the resulting grandchild has no controlling terminal.
Standard input is redirected from
.BR /dev/null ,
and standard output and error to the
.B \-\-log\-file
if given, or
.B /dev/null
otherwise.
.PP
Daemon mode requires
.BR fork (2)
and is therefore unavailable on Windows, where the program exits with an error.
Use a Windows service wrapper or run in the foreground instead.
.PP
When
.B \-\-pid\-file
is given, the daemon's PID is written there and the file is removed on clean
exit.
.B stop\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
uses it to send
.B SIGTERM
and, after a timeout,
.BR SIGKILL .
.SH START AND STOP SCRIPTS
.B start\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
and
.B stop\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
wrap the program for control-room use.
The start script reads
.B CRS_PORT_HTTP
(default 5002), forwards it as
.BR \-\-port ,
and runs the daemon with a PID file.
.PP
The configuration file may be given positionally \(em the form
.B crs\-app
uses \(em or with
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " or " \-\-config= \fIFILE\fR.
The port may be given with
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " or " \-\-port= \fIPORT\fR.
Any other option is forwarded to
.BR diskwatcher.py
unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-replace
Exit with an error if a copy is already running, instead of replacing it.
.TP
.BR \-\-run\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Per-node run directory instead of
.BR ./run/ \fIshort-hostname\fR.
Also settable as
.BR DW_RUN_DIR .
Passed to the daemon as
.BR \-\-run\-dir .
.TP
.BR \-\-pid\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
PID file to use instead of
.IR run-dir /mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.pid .
The same path is used both to look for a running copy and, as
.BR \-\-pid\-file ,
for the daemon to write, so the next start reads the file this one wrote.
Either way it overrides
.B watcher.pid_file
from the configuration file.
.SS Only one copy runs at a time
Before starting, the script stops any copy already running out of its own
directory.
Without this a second start would leave the first daemon holding the port while
the new one failed to bind, producing a restart that silently did not restart.
.PP
Both scripts look for a running copy in two places, because either alone has a
blind spot: the PID file misses a copy started by hand or one whose PID file was
deleted, while the process table misses a copy started from another directory.
.PP
A PID is acted on only once its command line confirms it is this application.
PID numbers are recycled, so a file left behind by a
.BR SIGKILL ed
daemon eventually names an unrelated process;
.BR kill (2)
signal 0 would report it alive, and signalling it would hit a stranger.
A candidate found in the process table must additionally have the script's own
directory as its working directory, so two checkouts on one host never stop each
other.
.PP
Shutdown is
.B SIGTERM
followed by
.B SIGKILL
after
.B CRS_STOP_TIMEOUT
seconds (default 10), which bounds how long a wedged daemon can block a restart.
.PP
The shared logic lives in
.BR ./lib/diskwatcher\-proc.sh ,
sourced by both scripts.
Reading another process's working directory needs
.BR lsof (8),
because
.B /proc
is Linux-only; where
.B lsof
is unavailable, process-table candidates are skipped rather than killed on a
guess, and the PID-file path continues to work.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The configuration file has four top-level keys:
.BR watcher ", " files ", " paths " and " peers .
This section summarises the schema; see
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.conf (5)
for the complete reference.
.SS watcher
Server settings:
.BR web_host ", " web_port ", " poll_interval ", " default_delay ", " daemon ", "
.BR run_dir ", " pid_file ", " log_file ", " peer_timeout ", " peer_interval .
.SS peers
A list of peer diskwatchers.
Each entry accepts
.BR url " (required), " label ", " timeout " and " enabled ,
or is a bare URL.
.SS files
A list of files to watch.
Each entry accepts
.BR path " (required), " delay ", " label ", " ssh " and " size .
.SS paths
A list of directories to watch.
Each entry accepts
.BR path " (required), " delay ", " label ", " ssh " and " space .
.PP
A directory's modification time changes when files are added to or removed from
it, but not when a file inside it is written.
To detect writes, list the file itself under
.BR files .
.SS delay
.B delay
is optional.
It is resolved as follows:
.PP
.nf
    delay: N          use N seconds
    delay: null       do not monitor staleness
    absent, but the entry has a space: or size: block
                      do not monitor staleness
    absent, nothing else
                      use watcher.default_delay (300 s)
.fi
.PP
The last case preserves the behaviour of earlier versions, so an existing
configuration keeps working unchanged.
.SH YAML FORMAT
.nf
watcher:
  web_host:      "0.0.0.0"
  web_port:      5002
  poll_interval: 30
  default_delay: 300
  daemon:        false
  run_dir:       "run/{host}"          # per node: the checkout is on NFS
  pid_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.pid"
  log_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.log"

files:
  # staleness only, exactly as in earlier versions
  - path:  /var/log/messages
    delay: 3600
    label: "System log"

  # staleness and size
  - path:  /data/mu2e/run_latest.dat
    delay: 120
    label: "Latest run data"
    size:
      warning:  "1 GiB"
      critical: "4 GiB"
      full:     "8 GiB"

  # alarm only if the file is ever zero length
  - path:  /data/mu2e/index.db
    label: "Run index"
    size:
      allow_empty: false

  # remote file; all ssh sub-keys except host are optional
  - path:  /data/mu2e/remote.dat
    delay: 120
    ssh:
      host:    daq-node01.example.com
      port:    22
      key:     ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
      timeout: 10
      options:
        - StrictHostKeyChecking=no

paths:
  # staleness and free space
  - path:  /data/mu2e
    delay: 300
    label: "Main data"
    space:
      warning:  "10%"
      critical: "500 GiB"
      full:     "50 GiB"

  # free space only; omitting delay disables the staleness alarm
  - path:  /scratch
    label: "Scratch volume"
    space:
      warning:  "2 TiB"
      critical: "500 GiB"
      full:     "50 GiB"

peers:
  # another diskwatcher whose entries are shown here too
  - url:   http://daq-node02.example.com:5002
    label: "daq-node02"
.fi
.SH WEB ENDPOINTS
.SS HTML pages
.TP
.B /
Watcher.
Every entry, with its modification time, age and staleness threshold.
Cards: OK, STALE, MISSING, UNMONITORED, TOTAL.
.TP
.B /space
Disk Space.
Directories with a
.B space
block, with free space against their thresholds.
Cards: GOOD, WARNING, CRITICAL, FULL, UNAVAILABLE, TOTAL.
.TP
.B /sizes
File Sizes.
Files with a
.B size
block.
Cards: GOOD, EMPTY, WARNING, CRITICAL, FULL, UNAVAILABLE, TOTAL.
.TP
.B /config
Settings in effect, the parsed watch list with its thresholds, any
configuration problems, and the raw YAML.
.TP
.B /api
Reference for every JSON endpoint and entry field.
.TP
.B /about
Version, uptime, host, and dependency versions.
.TP
.B /sitemap
The structure of the application.
.SS JSON endpoints
.TP
.B /api/status
All entries in the original flat shape.
See
.B COMPATIBILITY
below.
.TP
.B /api/state
Everything: all entries, the watch, space and size summaries, the version and
poll timing.
Preferred for new integrations.
.TP
.B /api/space
Directories with a
.B space
block, plus a summary.
.TP
.B /api/sizes
Files with a
.B size
block, plus a summary.
.TP
.B /api/entries
Filtered entry list.
Query parameters:
.BR kind = file | directory ,
.BR monitored = space | size ,
.BR state =\fISTATE\fR.
.TP
.B /api/peers
Connection status of every configured peer, without entries: label, URL,
.BR status " (" ok ", " error ", " pending " or " disabled ),
.BR error ,
the version and hostname the peer reports, fetch timing,
.BR source " (" static " or " discovered ),
and per-peer watch, space and size summaries.
.B counts
totals the connection states and
.B discovery
reports the last multicast scan: when, how many responders answered, how many
were set aside and why
.RB ( self ", " excluded ", a non-HTTP scheme)."
.TP
.B /api/config
Active settings, the parsed watch list, the peer list, and any configuration
problems.
.TP
.B /api/health
Poller liveness.
.B status
is
.B degraded
when the last poll is older than three poll intervals, or the configuration has
problems.
.B peers
carries the connection counts but does not affect
.BR status .
.TP
.B /api/version
Application name, version,
.B instance_id
and hostname.
.SS The ?peers=1 query parameter
.BR /api/status ", " /api/state ", " /api/space ", " /api/sizes " and "
.B /api/entries
return this instance's own entries by default.
With
.B ?peers=1
the first four add
.BR peers ,
one record per configured peer holding its connection details and its entries
filtered the way the endpoint filters local ones, plus
.BR aggregate ,
the endpoint's summary recomputed over local entries and every peer whose
.B status
is
.BR ok .
Entries retained from an unreachable peer are present under it with
.B stale
true but are not counted.
.B /api/entries?peers=1
folds reachable peers' entries into the flat list, each stamped with
.BR peer " and " peer_url ;
.B &peer=LABEL
keeps one peer's.
.SS Per-entry fields
Identity:
.BR path ", " label ", " kind ", " remote ", " ssh_host .
.PP
Staleness:
.BR delay ", " delay_str ", " mtime ", " mtime_str ", " age_s ", " age_str ", "
.BR stale ", " missing ", " error ", " watch_state ", " watch_rank .
.PP
Disk usage:
.BR disk_total ", " disk_used ", " disk_free ", " disk_pct " (percent used), "
.BR disk_free_pct ", " disk_total_str ", " disk_used_str ", " disk_free_str .
.PP
Free-space alarm:
.BR space_monitored ", " space_state ", " space_rank ", " space_trigger ", "
.BR space_reason ", " space_limits " (resolved to bytes), " space_limits_str
(as written in the YAML),
.BR space_limits_ordered .
.PP
Size alarm:
.BR size ", " size_str ", " size_monitored ", " size_state ", " size_rank ", "
.BR size_trigger ", " size_reason ", " size_limits ", " size_limits_str ", "
.BR size_pct ", " size_pct_ref .
.PP
Configuration:
.BR config_errors .
.PP
Origin (entries from a peer only):
.BR peer ", " peer_url .
.PP
The
.B *_rank
fields carry a numeric severity, higher being worse.
Sort on those rather than re-deriving an ordering from the state names.
.SS Compatibility
.B /api/status
always returns
.BR files ", " total ", " stale ", " ok " and " poll_interval ,
and every element of
.B files
always carries at least
.BR path ", " label ", " delay ", " kind ", " remote ", " ssh_host ", " mtime ", "
.BR mtime_str ", " age_s ", " age_str ", " delay_str ", " stale ", " missing ", "
.BR error ", " disk_total ", " disk_used ", " disk_free ", " disk_pct ", "
.BR disk_total_str " and " disk_free_str .
Keys are added, never removed or retyped.
The single documented exception is that
.B delay
may now be
.B null
for an entry that opts out of staleness monitoring.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./diskwatcher.yaml
Default configuration file.
.TP
.B ./config/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.yaml
Configuration used by the control-room start script.
.TP
.B ./diskwatcher.py
Entry-point shim invoked by the start script.
.TP
.B ./src/mu2edaq_diskwatcher/
The application package.
.TP
.B ./pyproject.toml
Packaging metadata; installs the
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher
console script.
.TP
.B ./run/\fIshort-hostname\fR/
Per-node run directory: pid file, log, and any future persistent state.
.TP
.B ./diskwatcher.pid
Where releases before 1.3.0 wrote the pid file; still read by the start and
stop scripts.
.TP
.B ./start\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
Control-room start script; replaces any copy already running.
.TP
.B ./stop\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
Control-room stop script.
.TP
.B ./lib/diskwatcher\-proc.sh
Process discovery sourced by both scripts.
.TP
.B ./CHANGELOG.md
What changed in each release, including changes affecting an existing
configuration file or a program reading the JSON API.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run in the foreground on the default port:
.PP
.nf
    mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-\-config config/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.yaml
.fi
.PP
Run as a daemon on port 8080 with the pid file and log under
.B run/\fIshort-hostname\fR/
(the defaults):
.PP
.nf
    mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-c watch.yaml \-p 8080 \-\-daemon
.fi
.PP
Keep the runtime files on node-local disk instead, still keyed by node and
port so several instances cannot collide:
.PP
.nf
    mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-c watch.yaml \-\-daemon \\
        \-\-run\-dir /var/run/diskwatcher\-{host}\-{port}
.fi
.PP
Override the port from the environment instead:
.PP
.nf
    MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_WEB_PORT=8080 mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-c watch.yaml
.fi
.PP
Restart the control-room daemon.
The running copy is stopped automatically, so no separate stop is needed:
.PP
.nf
    ./start\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh \-c config/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.yaml
.fi
.PP
Start only if nothing is running, leaving an existing daemon alone:
.PP
.nf
    ./start\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh \-\-no\-replace || echo "already running"
.fi
.PP
List every volume in a CRITICAL or FULL state:
.PP
.nf
    curl \-s localhost:5002/api/space | python3 \-c \\
      'import json,sys; print([e["path"] for e in json.load(sys.stdin)["entries"]
       if e["space_state"] in ("CRITICAL","FULL")])'
.fi
.PP
List every zero-length file:
.PP
.nf
    curl \-s "localhost:5002/api/entries?state=EMPTY"
.fi
.PP
Check that the poller is alive, for use in a monitoring script:
.PP
.nf
    test "$(curl \-fsS localhost:5002/api/health | \\
        python3 \-c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["status"])')" = ok
.fi
.PP
Show two other nodes' entries on this dashboard as well, without editing the
configuration file:
.PP
.nf
    mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-c watch.yaml \-\-peer mu2e\-dl\-01:5002 \-\-peer mu2e\-dl\-02:5002
.fi
.PP
Federate every diskwatcher on the data-logger nodes that answers discovery:
.PP
.nf
    mu2edaq\-diskwatcher \-c watch.yaml \-\-discover\-peers \-\-discover\-filter 'host=mu2e\-dl\-*'
.fi
.PP
List the peers that are currently unreachable:
.PP
.nf
    curl \-s localhost:5002/api/peers | python3 \-c \\
      'import json,sys; print([p["label"] for p in json.load(sys.stdin)["peers"]
       if p["status"] != "ok"])'
.fi
.PP
Every CRITICAL volume here or on any reachable peer:
.PP
.nf
    curl \-s "localhost:5002/api/entries?peers=1&monitored=space&state=CRITICAL"
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration file was named explicitly \(em by
.B \-\-config
or by
.B MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_CONFIG
\(em but does not exist, or daemon mode was requested on a platform without
.BR fork (2).
.PP
Configuration problems within a file that does exist are reported as warnings
and do not prevent startup: a control-room daemon keeps running on a partly
bad configuration rather than leaving the experiment unmonitored.
.PP
The start script exits
.B 1
when the configuration file does not exist, or when a copy is already running
and
.B \-\-no\-replace
was given; and
.B 2
when an option is missing its argument.
The stop script exits
.B 0
whether or not anything was running.
